rtcuts  there's no easy way to do it you have to  make a decision within yourself to say I  am going to make a change so that I can  get the results that I want that makes  me feel good about me okay so Adrienne  here is talking about making sacrifices  like giving up foods that you truly love  in order to achieve your weight loss  goal I don't know I think this whole  insistence on extreme sacrifice is the  problematic concept in of itself I  strongly believe that weight loss does  not have to mean cutting out foods that  you genuinely enjoy and she clearly  loves bread she talks about loving bread  in fact by classifying a food that you  love as a forbidden or bad recent  suggests that we just kind of tend to  obsess over it and want it more often  resulting in a binge this is not the  sustainable lifestyle that Adrienne  talked about wanting for herself it is  also perfectly possible to include foods  that you actually love in your diet  while still having a weight loss school  and enjoying them in moderation honestly  the idea that not giving something that  means you don't want it badly enough is  so heavily ingrained in diet culture and  is riddled with victim blaming and guilt  and nobody needs that let's see what  else and I would drink a gallon of water  a day for me it was amazing I felt a  difference in my skin moments that I had  my crazy cravings I would drink hot  water with lemon and like that would  take the craving away or I would just  sit in like chug water  no I don't I just eaten you know when  you finish like a healthy meal and  you're like but I'm still hungry I've  been there drink water drink hot water  with lemon that is the key so I'm happy  to hear that Adrienne says she felt  great drinking a gallon of water I mean  if that's what your body needs  especially if you're running around  performing that might all means drink up  I mean listen to your body and pay  attention to those early signs of thirst  like dry mouth or concentrated urine