ching your ultimate goal or doesn't  support you or encourage you you might  want to cut those people off okay so  what Adrienne is describing here is one  of the many reasons why dieting is so  hard for so many people for one food and  eating is a social phenomenon right we  often eat our meals around the dinner  table with our families or at social  gatherings at restaurants with friends  or coworkers we celebrate with food and  we mourn with food however what often  happens with dieting is that it can  inhibit these social activities either  because maybe the restaurant doesn't  meet our specific dietary restrictions  or an Adrienne's case because of the  perceived lack of support or food  shaming you get from friends I can  really empathize with Adrienne as it is  not nice for your friends to mock you  and not support your goals however I  think one question that I would want to  ask myself before I would go to the  extreme of cutting someone out is is my  diet or weight loss really worth losing  a friend so perhaps before making any  concrete decisions around phasing out  friend groups it might be helpful to  have an open conversation with your  friends or family and she mentions doing  this so let them know that this goal is  important to you and explain how they  can better support you so that you can  feel comfortable in social gatherings  that involve food also I would make sure  it's clear that you are not judging  their eating or pushing your diet agenda  on them as that is often a reason for  kind of the mocking or the pushback if  this is something that you're struggling  with I go into a lot more detail on diet  shaming in my video right here and in my  minds your own plate blog post that I'm  gonna be leaving in the description  below so let's see what else she has to  say self-discipline is sexy before  eating like the whole breadbasket I'd be  like damn self-discipline is sexy  because when you recognize how strong  your willpower is and how strong 