er there is  actually very little research to support  consuming a vegan diet for Hashimoto's  disease or hypothyroidism we did find  one 2013 study which suggested that a  vegan diet may decrease the risk of  hypothyroidism however the results of  the study were not even statistically  significant one major concern that I  want to point out for individuals with  Hashimoto's disease or hypothyroidism  who may be following a vegan diet is the  importance of iodine iodine is an  essential nutrient necessary for thyroid  hormone production and individuals  following a strictly vegan diet may be  at higher risk of iodine deficiency due  to lower intakes of dairy and seafood  now some vegan friendly sources of  iodine that you definitely would want to  consider including in your diet include  seaweed iodized salt whole grain bread  prunes and lima beans again I'm not  saying that veganism causes iodine  deficiency I'm just saying that  statistically there is a slightly higher  risk I also want to mention that there  are some foods that may be beneficial  for some individuals with thyroid  dysfunction to limit so there are some  compounds called boi Trojans which may  have a negative effect on the thyroid by  interfering with some of the uptake of  iodine so some examples of goitrogens in  common foods include cruciferous  vegetables like broccoli bok choy  brussel sprouts and cabbage as well as  soy based foods like tofu tempeh edamame  and soy milk this is especially  important to consider in the context of  a vegan diet as it may rely heavily on  some of these types of foods you likely  don't have to  eliminate these foods but it is  something to be mindful that may be  problematic when consumed raw in the  case of the cruciferous vegetables or in  excess now the goitrogens and soy may  not be a huge issue for Adrienne but one  thing I am concerned about is her  caloric intake low caloric intakes have  been associated with decreased thyroid  hormones like t3 and t4 with research on  chronic starvation and restrictions  suggesting a very close relationship  between low food intake and abnormal  thyroid function so this is important to  consider if you're following a  particularly restrictive diet and are  susceptible to thyroid dysfunction we're  going to be talking a lot more about  Hashimoto's and hypothyroidism and  nutrition in a video that I'm working on  right now so definitely make sure that  you are subscribed so that you do not  miss out now all that said you know that  I think there are lots of reasons to eat  a vegan diet and I do not think that  hypothyroidism is a reason to  necessarily avoid it  so as long as eating plant-based makes  Adrienne feel better amazing this is  what I want to hear now overall I am  getting the impression that Adrienne has  sadly fallen into the deep end of a lot  of diet culture traps for instance we  see that she tends to fill up on a lot  of fluid rather than eating solid food  and any solid meal that she does have  tends to be very low in calories and  void of really important macro nutrients  like protein and carbohydrates we also  see a preoccupation with detoxing weight  loss avoiding sugar cravings drinking  lots of water and practicing  self-discipline to restrict calories  now while having a weight loss goal is a  personal choice that I totally support I  am concerned with the excessive level of  restriction that Adrienne is putting her  body through to achieve this goal  even though we only witnessed one full  day of eating if Adrienne is regularly  eating only 50% of her daily energy  needs while exercising at a pretty elite  level  I do have concerns about the long-term  impact of the severe restriction  metabolically reproductively and  psychologically listen I have a lot of  empathy for anyone working in Hollywood  who is not naturally a size-two it is an  unfair reality that a lot of really  talented people will have to fight extra  hard at the expense of their long-term  he